Implement deepClone(value) in deepClone.js (ES module). Handles primitives, plain objects, arrays, Date, and Map. No JSON.stringify, no structuredClone.
Implement `deepClone(value)` in `deepClone.js`. The function must handle:

- Primitives (number, string, boolean, null, undefined): return as-is
- Plain objects `{}`: deep copy all own enumerable properties
- Arrays `[]`: deep copy all elements
- `Date` instances: return `new Date(original.getTime())`
- `Map` instances: return a new `Map` with deep-cloned keys and values
- Nested combinations of the above

## Constraints
- No external dependencies (Node.js stdlib only)
- Must use `export default deepClone` (ES module)
- Do NOT use `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(...))` (it breaks Dates and Maps)
- Do NOT use `structuredClone` (implement manually)

## Test contract
```js
const obj = { a: 1, b: { c: [2, 3] }, d: new Date(0), e: new Map([["x", {y: 9}]]) };
const clone = deepClone(obj);
clone.b.c.push(99);
clone.e.get("x").y = 999;
// original must be unaffected
assert(obj.b.c.length === 2);
assert(obj.e.get("x").y === 9);
assert(clone.d.getTime() === 0);
```

Implement DependencyResolver and CyclicDependencyError in dep_resolver.js as ES module. Topological sort via DFS with grey/black cycle detection. resolve() and resolveAll() return packages in installation order. No duplicates.
Write `dep_resolver.ts` (or `dep_resolver.js` as ESM) implementing a dependency resolver.

## Interface
```typescript
type DependencyGraph = Record<string, string[]>;
// key = package name, value = list of direct dependencies

class DependencyResolver {
  constructor(graph: DependencyGraph);

  resolve(entryPoint: string): string[];
  // Returns packages in installation order (topological sort):
  // dependencies before dependents.
  // Throws Error if entryPoint not in graph.
  // Throws CyclicDependencyError (extends Error) if cycle detected.

  resolveAll(): string[];
  // Resolve entire graph. Returns topo-sorted list of all packages.
  // Throws CyclicDependencyError if any cycle detected.
}

class CyclicDependencyError extends Error {
  cycle: string[]; // e.g. ["a", "b", "c", "a"]
}
```

## Algorithm
- Use DFS with grey/black coloring to detect cycles
- Grey = currently in DFS stack (cycle detected if revisited)
- Black = fully processed
- Output order: a package appears after all its dependencies

## Test assertions
```typescript
import { DependencyResolver, CyclicDependencyError } from './dep_resolver.js';

// Simple chain: c -> b -> a (a has no deps)
const g1 = { a: [], b: ["a"], c: ["b"] };
const r1 = new DependencyResolver(g1);
const order1 = r1.resolve("c");
const aIdx = order1.indexOf("a"), bIdx = order1.indexOf("b"), cIdx = order1.indexOf("c");
console.assert(aIdx < bIdx, "a before b");
console.assert(bIdx < cIdx, "b before c");
console.assert(order1.includes("a") && order1.includes("b") && order1.includes("c"));

// Diamond dependency
const g2 = { a: [], b: ["a"], c: ["a"], d: ["b", "c"] };
const r2 = new DependencyResolver(g2);
const order2 = r2.resolve("d");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("a") < order2.indexOf("b"), "a before b in diamond");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("a") < order2.indexOf("c"), "a before c in diamond");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("b") < order2.indexOf("d"), "b before d in diamond");
console.assert(order2.indexOf("c") < order2.indexOf("d"), "c before d in diamond");
// No duplicates
console.assert(new Set(order2).size === order2.length, "no duplicates");

// Cycle detection
const g3 = { a: ["b"], b: ["c"], c: ["a"] };
const r3 = new DependencyResolver(g3);
try {
  r3.resolve("a");
  console.assert(false, "should throw CyclicDependencyError");
} catch (e) {
  console.assert(e instanceof CyclicDependencyError, "should be CyclicDependencyError");
  console.assert(Array.isArray(e.cycle) && e.cycle.length >= 3, "cycle path present");
}

// resolveAll
const g4 = { a: [], b: ["a"], c: ["a"], d: [] };
const r4 = new DependencyResolver(g4);
const all = r4.resolveAll();
console.assert(all.length === 4, "all 4 packages");
console.assert(all.indexOf("a") < all.indexOf("b"), "a before b in resolveAll");

console.log("ALL TESTS PASSED");
```

## Constraints
- No external packages — Node.js stdlib only
- Single file `dep_resolver.ts` or `dep_resolver.js` (ES module with named exports)

Implement StateMachine class in state_machine.ts/js as ES module. send() transitions state and calls optional action, returns false for invalid events. canSend() checks if transition exists. Must pass all test assertions.
Write `state_machine.ts` (or `state_machine.js` as ESM) implementing a generic `StateMachine` class.

## Interface
```typescript
type Transition<S extends string, E extends string> = {
  from: S;
  event: E;
  to: S;
  action?: () => void;
};

class StateMachine<S extends string, E extends string> {
  constructor(initialState: S, transitions: Transition<S, E>[]);

  getState(): S;

  send(event: E): boolean;
  // Returns true if transition succeeded, false if no valid transition exists from current state + event.
  // If a valid transition has an action, call it before updating state.

  canSend(event: E): boolean;
  // Returns true if there is a valid transition for current state + event.
}
```

## Example (traffic light)
```typescript
const light = new StateMachine("red", [
  { from: "red",    event: "next", to: "green"  },
  { from: "green",  event: "next", to: "yellow" },
  { from: "yellow", event: "next", to: "red"    },
]);

light.getState(); // "red"
light.send("next"); // true
light.getState(); // "green"
```

## Test assertions (must all pass)
```typescript
import { StateMachine } from './state_machine.js';

// Traffic light
const light = new StateMachine("red", [
  { from: "red",    event: "next", to: "green"  },
  { from: "green",  event: "next", to: "yellow" },
  { from: "yellow", event: "next", to: "red"    },
]);

console.assert(light.getState() === "red");
console.assert(light.send("next") === true);
console.assert(light.getState() === "green");
console.assert(light.send("next") === true);
console.assert(light.getState() === "yellow");
console.assert(light.send("invalid") === false);  // unknown event
console.assert(light.getState() === "yellow");     // no state change

// canSend
console.assert(light.canSend("next") === true);
console.assert(light.canSend("invalid") === false);

// Action callback
let actionCalled = false;
const door = new StateMachine("closed", [
  { from: "closed", event: "open",  to: "open",   action: () => { actionCalled = true; } },
  { from: "open",   event: "close", to: "closed" },
]);
door.send("open");
console.assert(actionCalled === true, "action should be called");
console.assert(door.getState() === "open");

console.log("ALL TESTS PASSED");
```

## Constraints
- TypeScript or plain ES module JavaScript
- No external packages — Node.js stdlib
- Single file `state_machine.ts` or `state_machine.js` (ES module with `export`)
- No runtime type-checking libraries
